# Code Style

## Error Handling

Always wrap operations in try/catch. Error messages must follow:

```
[filename: functionName] Description of what went wrong || relevantVariable=value || Original error message
```

```typescript
try {
  const user = await getUser(userId);
} catch (error) {
  throw new Error(
    `[user.service: getUser] Failed to fetch user || userId=${userId} || ${error.message}`,
  );
}
```

## JSDoc

Add JSDoc to every function, class, method, and type. Explain clearly and simply.

```typescript
/**
 * Fetches a user by their ID from the database.
 * @param userId - The unique identifier of the user
 * @returns The user object if found
 * @throws Error if the user does not exist
 */
```

## Console Logging Format

When asked to add `console.log` statements, use this format and pick a random animal emoji each time:

```typescript
console.log("[functionName | what we are checking | 🦊]", theThingToLog);
```

Examples:

- `console.log('[getUser | fetched user data | 🐸]', user);`
- `console.log('[syncCampaigns | campaign count | 🦉]', campaigns.length);`
- `console.log('[validateToken | token expiry | 🐙]', token.exp);`

The animal-emoji `console.log` format is for local debugging — strip it before committing. Committed backend code uses Pino only (`request.log.*` or `fastify.log.*`).
